### Data Preparation

The first step is to load the required R packages and import the NYCHA electricity dataset.

```{r setup, echo=TRUE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE}

library(readr)
library(dplyr)
library(ggplot2)
library(knitr)

nycha <- read_csv(
  "Data /NYCHA_Electric_Consumption_And_Cost.csv",
  show_col_types = FALSE
)

```

The dataset is imported into R and stored in an object named `nycha`.

### Research question

How is electricity consumption associated with electricity cost among NYCHA housing developments in New York City, and how does this relationship vary across boroughs?

This question is appropriate because the dataset contains electricity billing records from NYCHA developments. The results will describe the observed NYCHA records and cannot be generalized to all buildings in New York City.

### Cases

The cases are monthly electricity billing records for NYCHA housing developments. Each row represents an electricity record for a development during a specific billing period.

```{r cases, echo=TRUE}

nycha_cases <- nycha |>
  transmute(
    development_name = `Development Name`,
    borough = Borough,
    revenue_month = `Revenue Month`,
    consumption_kwh = parse_number(
      as.character(`Consumption (KWH)`)
    ),
    current_charges = parse_number(
      as.character(`Current Charges`)
    )
  )

cases_summary <- nycha_cases |>
  summarise(
    total_cases = n(),
    total_developments = n_distinct(development_name),
    total_boroughs = n_distinct(borough)
  )

kable(
  cases_summary,
  caption = "Summary of NYCHA Cases"
)

```

This output shows the total number of billing records, NYCHA developments, and boroughs represented in the dataset.

### Data collection

The data were collected from administrative electricity billing records for New York City Housing Authority developments.

The records include information about electricity consumption, electricity charges, housing developments, boroughs, and billing periods. The data were not collected through a controlled experiment or a survey conducted for this project.

### Type of study

This is an observational study.

The electricity consumption and charges were recorded naturally through the NYCHA billing system. The researcher did not assign electricity usage, electricity costs, boroughs, or billing periods.

Therefore, this analysis can describe associations between electricity consumption and electricity cost, but it cannot establish causation.

### Data Source

The data were obtained from NYC Open Data.

**Dataset:** Electric Consumption and Cost  
**Publisher:** New York City Housing Authority  
**Source:** [NYC Open Data — Electric Consumption and Cost](https://data.cityofnewyork.us/d/jr24-e7cr)

The original CSV file is stored in:

```text
Data /NYCHA_Electric_Consumption_And_Cost.csv
```

### Describe your variables

The following variables will be used in this analysis:

- `development_name`: the name of the NYCHA development. This is a qualitative variable.
- `borough`: the New York City borough. This is a qualitative variable.
- `revenue_month`: the billing month. This is a time variable.
- `consumption_kwh`: electricity consumption measured in kilowatt-hours. This is a quantitative variable.
- `current_charges`: current electricity charges measured in U.S. dollars. This is a quantitative variable.
- `kwh_charges`: charges associated with electricity consumption. This is a quantitative variable.

The dependent variable is `current_charges` because the analysis examines electricity cost.

The main explanatory variable is `consumption_kwh`.

The variable `borough` will be used to compare electricity consumption and cost across the five boroughs.

```{r variables, echo=TRUE}

nycha_clean <- nycha |>
  transmute(
    development_name = `Development Name`,
    borough = Borough,
    revenue_month = `Revenue Month`,
    consumption_kwh = parse_number(
      as.character(`Consumption (KWH)`)
    ),
    current_charges = parse_number(
      as.character(`Current Charges`)
    ),
    kwh_charges = parse_number(
      as.character(`KWH Charges`)
    )
  ) |>
  mutate(
    borough = toupper(trimws(borough))
  )

glimpse(nycha_clean)

```

This output confirms the structure and data types of the variables used in the analysis.

### Relevant summary statistics

The following chunk calculates summary statistics for electricity consumption and current electricity charges.

```{r summary-statistics, echo=TRUE}

summary_statistics <- nycha_clean |>
  summarise(
    observations = n(),

    mean_consumption = mean(
      consumption_kwh,
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),

    median_consumption = median(
      consumption_kwh,
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),

    sd_consumption = sd(
      consumption_kwh,
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),

    minimum_consumption = min(
      consumption_kwh,
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),

    maximum_consumption = max(
      consumption_kwh,
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),

    mean_cost = mean(
      current_charges,
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),

    median_cost = median(
      current_charges,
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),

    sd_cost = sd(
      current_charges,
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),

    minimum_cost = min(
      current_charges,
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),

    maximum_cost = max(
      current_charges,
      na.rm = TRUE
    )
  )

kable(
  summary_statistics,
  digits = 2,
  caption = "Summary Statistics for Electricity Consumption and Cost"
)

```

This table reports the number of observations, mean, median, standard deviation, minimum, and maximum for electricity consumption and electricity cost.

### Summary statistics by borough

The following chunk compares electricity consumption and electricity cost across boroughs.

```{r borough-summary, echo=TRUE}

borough_summary <- nycha_clean |>
  filter(
    !is.na(borough),
    !is.na(consumption_kwh),
    !is.na(current_charges)
  ) |>
  group_by(borough) |>
  summarise(
    observations = n(),

    average_consumption = mean(
      consumption_kwh,
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),

    median_consumption = median(
      consumption_kwh,
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),

    average_cost = mean(
      current_charges,
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),

    median_cost = median(
      current_charges,
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),

    .groups = "drop"
  ) |>
  arrange(desc(average_consumption))

kable(
  borough_summary,
  digits = 2,
  caption = "Electricity Consumption and Cost by Borough"
)

```

This table shows how the typical electricity consumption and electricity cost differ among the five boroughs.

### Visualization: Electricity consumption and cost

The following chunk creates a scatter plot to examine the relationship between electricity consumption and electricity cost.

```{r consumption-cost-plot, echo=TRUE}

ggplot(
  nycha_clean |>
    filter(
      !is.na(consumption_kwh),
      !is.na(current_charges)
    ),
  aes(
    x = consumption_kwh,
    y = current_charges,
    color = borough
  )
) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.4) +
  geom_smooth(
    method = "lm",
    se = FALSE
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "Electricity Consumption and Cost by Borough",
    x = "Electricity consumption (kWh)",
    y = "Current charges (USD)",
    color = "Borough"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()

```

Each point represents one electricity billing record. The plot shows whether higher electricity consumption tends to be associated with higher electricity cost.

### Visualization: Electricity cost by borough

The following chunk creates a boxplot to compare electricity charges across boroughs.

```{r cost-boxplot, echo=TRUE}

ggplot(
  nycha_clean |>
    filter(
      !is.na(borough),
      !is.na(current_charges)
    ),
  aes(
    x = borough,
    y = current_charges,
    fill = borough
  )
) +
  geom_boxplot(
    alpha = 0.7,
    outlier.alpha = 0.25
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "Distribution of Electricity Charges by Borough",
    x = "Borough",
    y = "Current charges (USD)"
  ) +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(
    legend.position = "none"
  )

```

This boxplot compares the median, variability, and possible outliers of electricity charges for each borough.

### Proposed regression model

A linear regression model may be used to analyze the association between electricity consumption and electricity cost.

```{r regression-model, echo=TRUE}

cost_model <- lm(
  current_charges ~ consumption_kwh + borough,
  data = nycha_clean
)

summary(cost_model)

```

In this model, `current_charges` is the dependent variable. `consumption_kwh` and `borough` are the explanatory variables.

The model will describe the association between electricity consumption, borough, and electricity cost. Because the study is observational, the results will not be interpreted as proof of causation.